      "slide_id": "masterclass-1",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "1 · Make it rough, make it early",
      "sentence": "A rough prototype is a question in material form. Make the smallest version that can teach you something.",
      "quote": "A rough thing can answer a question that a polished thing hides.",
      "quote_origin": "tao_invented",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Tao of Creativity)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-3-development-solutions/#tao-of-creativity"
      },
      "image_brief": "Antoni Gaudí's hanging string-and-weight model for the Colonia Güell church, photographed c. 1908: a rough model built early to ask a structural question in material form.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Maqueta polifunicular.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U3.masterclass-1",
      "notes": "- A rough prototype is a question in material form.\n- It does not need to look finished. It needs to reveal one thing you do not know yet.\n- Build the smallest version that lets a classmate react.\n- \"The prototype is meant to answer questions, so keep it focused.\"\nSource: the prototype quote is (Knapp, Zeratsky, and Kowitz 2016, chap. 13); the lesson also cites (Cross 2006, vi) for design ability as resolving ill-defined problems. Other bullets and the slide's Tao line are course wording.\nAsk: What can this rough object tell you that a description cannot?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The prototype is meant to answer questions, so keep it focused.",
          "cite": "Knapp, Zeratsky, and Kowitz 2016, chap. 13"
        },
        {
          "text": "I summarised design ability as comprising abilities of resolving ill-defined problems, adopting solution-focused cognitive strategies",
          "cite": "Cross 2006, vi"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-2",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "2 · Defer judgement",
      "sentence": "Protect a short space for variation before you decide what works. Then judge with a named criterion.",
      "image_brief": "Beethoven's sketchbook for the Seventh Symphony, 1812: themes tried in several versions before any is judged final — a protected space for variation before decision.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Sketches for the second movement of Symphony no. 7, op. 92, Beethoven, Petter Sketchbook, 1812, musical autograph - Morgan Library & Museum - New York City - DSC06691.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U3.masterclass-2",
      "notes": "- Early critique can close a path before it shows what it might become.\n- Protect a short space for variation first. Then judge with a named criterion.\n- This is not permission to keep every idea. It stops you mistaking the first awkward version for the final limit.\n- Osborn's first rule: criticism is withheld until all ideas are in. In an experiment, college students who expected their artworks to be evaluated produced work judged less creative.\nSource: the rule is (Osborn 1942, chap. 4); the evaluation result is (Amabile 1979, 221). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: Which idea did you drop too early this week?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Judicial judgment is ruled out. Criticism must be withheld until all ideas are in.",
          "cite": "Osborn 1942, chap. 4"
        },
        {
          "text": "subjects in the evaluation groups produced artworks significantly lower on judged creativity than did subjects in the nonevaluation control groups",
          "cite": "Amabile 1979, 221"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-3",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "diagram",
      "heading": "3 · Iterate with an exit",
      "sentence": "Write the test before the next version. Stop when the test is answered.",
      "image_brief": "Replica of the Wright brothers' 1901 wind tunnel: wing shapes tested against one question before the next glider was built, and testing stopped when the data answered it.",
      "notes": "- Revision helps when you know what the next version must answer.\n- Write one test before you change the work: \"Can the viewer find the main action?\" or \"Does the form fit the brief?\"\n- Stop when the test is answered, even if another improvement is possible.\n- Iteration can improve an idea, but it can also cause fixation: refining one option without considering others.\nSource: the iteration/fixation bullet is (Dow et al. 2010, 18:1). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: What is the one test your next version must pass?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Iteration can help people improve ideas. It can also give rise to fixation, continuously refining one option without considering others.",
          "cite": "Dow et al. 2010, 18:1"
        },
        {
          "text": "ads created in the Parallel condition significantly outperformed those from the Serial condition. Moreover, independent raters found Parallel prototypes to be more diverse. Parallel participants also reported a larger increase in task-specific self-confidence.",
          "cite": "Dow et al. 2010, 18:1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-4",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "diagram",
      "heading": "4 · Sketch to think",
      "sentence": "Three quick arrangements can show relationships that one polished drawing hides.",
      "quote": "Sketching enables exploration of the problem space and the solution space to proceed together.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Cross 2006, 37)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-3-development-solutions/#ref-cross-2006"
      },
      "image_brief": "A sheet of quick alternative sketches by a designer or artist — several arrangements of one idea side by side — showing sketching as a way of thinking before anything is built.",
      "notes": "- Draw three different arrangements, not one beautiful drawing.\n- Cross: \"Sketching enables exploration of the problem space and the solution space to proceed together.\"\n- Compare relationships, rhythm and emphasis. A sketch is a temporary argument: it shows what your idea is doing.\nSource: the quote is (Cross 2006, 37). The first and third bullets are course wording.\nAsk: What did the second and third arrangement show that the first one hid?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Sketching enables exploration of the problem space and the solution space to proceed together",
          "cite": "Cross 2006, 37"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-5",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "5 · Open the solution space",
      "sentence": "Reframe an unclear brief as two or three questions. Make one small response to each.",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Beghetto and Karwowski 2025, 3)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-3-development-solutions/#ref-beghetto-karwowski-2025"
      },
      "image_brief": "Paul Klee, a page of the Pedagogical Sketchbook (1925): one line or form opened into several possible developments, a bounded way to widen the solution space.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Paul Klee Pädagogisches Skizzenbuch 10.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U3.masterclass-5",
      "notes": "- Some briefs are unclear because the problem itself is still moving. Do not force one path too soon.\n- Reframe the brief as two or three possible questions. Make one small response to each.\n- Creative agency: \"the intentional and self-directed capacity to envision and enact new and meaningful actions within the existing constraints of a particular context\".\n- Every way of stating a wicked problem already points to a solution.\nSource: the wicked-problem bullet is (Buchanan 1992, 16); the creative-agency definition is (Beghetto and Karwowski 2025, 3). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: Which of your questions surprised you most?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "every formulation of a wicked problem corresponds to the formulation of a solution",
          "cite": "Buchanan 1992, 16"
        },
        {
          "text": "The intentional and self-directed capacity to envision and enact new and meaningful actions within the existing constraints of a particular context",
          "cite": "Beghetto and Karwowski 2025, 3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-6",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "6 · Reflect at checkpoints",
      "sentence": "Write what changed, what stayed, and what the change taught you.",
      "image_brief": "Orville Wright's diary entry for 17 December 1903: a written record of what was tried, what happened and what was learned — reflection at a checkpoint.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Wright diary.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U3.masterclass-6",
      "notes": "- Pause after each version. Write three lines: what changed, what stayed, what the change taught you.\n- Schön: the situation \"talks back\", and in good design this conversation with the situation is reflective.\n- The checkpoint turns attempts into evidence you can discuss. It is a teaching prompt, not a model of every studio decision.\nSource: the Schön bullet is (Schön 1983, 79). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: What did your last checkpoint teach you?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "the situation 'talks back,' and he responds to the situation's back-talk. In a good process of design, this conversation with the situation is reflective.",
          "cite": "Schön 1983, 79"
        }
      ]
    },
